}\n\\tableofcontents\n}\n\\section{Cybersecurity Fundamentals: Architecture, Cryptography, and\nDefense\nMechanisms}\\label{cybersecurity-fundamentals-architecture-cryptography-and-defense-mechanisms}\n\n\\subsection{1. Learning Objectives}\\label{learning-objectives}\n\nBy the conclusion of this chapter, the reader will be able to:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\def\\labelenumi{\\arabic{enumi}.}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Articulate} the core pillars of information security (CIA\n  triad) and apply them to organizational risk management.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Differentiate} and implement various cryptographic primitives,\n  including symmetric/asymmetric encryption and hashing, within secure\n  system designs.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Evaluate} web and network vulnerabilities using standard\n  frameworks (OWASP, NIST) and apply mitigation strategies.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Design} secure access control architectures based on Identity\n  and Access Management (IAM) principles, including Zero Trust\n  implementation.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Develop} a Security Operations plan that integrates Business\n  Continuity, Disaster Recovery, and Compliance Auditing.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{2. Executive Overview}\\label{executive-overview}\n\nIn the modern digital landscape, information security is no longer a\nperipheral technical function but a critical business enabler. As\norganizations migrate to cloud environments, adopt interconnected IoT\necosystems, and conduct remote operations, the attack surface expands\nexponentially. This expansion creates a complex environment where data\nbreaches, service outages, and regulatory non-compliance carry severe\nfinancial and reputational consequences.\n\nThis domain addresses the necessity of embedding security into the\nlifecycle of every technology asset. It moves beyond simple perimeter\ndefense to a holistic approach that encompasses cryptography, identity\nmanagement, network architecture, and application security.\nUnderstanding the theoretical foundations (such as the CIA triad) allows\npractitioners to make informed decisions when selecting tools and\nprotocols (e.g., TLS vs.~HTTP, OAuth vs.~Basic Auth). Furthermore, this\nchapter bridges the gap between technical implementation and strategic\ngovernance, covering compliance frameworks like ISO 27001 and NIST CSF.\n\n\\subsection{3. Core Concepts \\& Technical\nDefinitions}\\label{core-concepts-technical-definitions}\n\n\\textbf{Information Security (InfoSec):} The practice of protecting\ninformation systems, networks, and data from unauthorized access, theft,\nor damage. It ensures that data remains safe while maintaining\noperational continuity.\n\n\\textbf{CIA Triad:} The foundational model of information security\nconsisting of \\textbf{Confidentiality} (access restricted to authorized\nusers only), \\textbf{Integrity} (data is accurate and unaltered), and\n\\textbf{Availability} (systems are accessible when needed).\n\n\\textbf{Threat:} A potential danger to the information system, such as a\nhacker group or a natural disaster. A threat becomes real only when\nexploited.\n\n\\textbf{Vulnerability:} A weakness or flaw in a system (e.g., unpatched\nsoftware, weak password policy) that a threat actor can exploit to\nbreach security.\n\n\\textbf{Risk:} The likelihood of a threat exploiting a vulnerability\nmultiplied by the impact of the resulting damage. Risk is calculated\nusing \\(Risk = Threat \\times Vulnerability \\times Impact\\).\n\n\\textbf{Security Control:} A policy, procedure, or technology (e.g.,\nfirewall, encryption) implemented to mitigate risk. Controls are\ncategorized as preventative, detective, or corrective.\n\n\\textbf{Zero Trust Architecture:} A security strategy that assumes the\nnetwork is compromised and verifies every request regardless of its\norigin. It operates on the principle of “never trust, always verify.”\n\n\\textbf{Defense in Depth:} A strategy that layers multiple security\ncontrols throughout the network and application stack to ensure that if\none layer fails, others remain intact to protect data.\n\n\\subsection{4. In-Depth Subject Matter\nCoverage}\\label{in-depth-subject-matter-coverage}\n\n\\subsubsection{4.1 Cryptography\nFundamentals}\\label{cryptography-fundamentals}\n\nCryptography is the science of secure communication. It provides the\nmathematical guarantees for confidentiality and integrity in digital\nsystems.\n\n\\paragraph{4.1.1 Symmetric and Asymmetric\nEncryption}\\label{symmetric-and-asymmetric-encryption}\n\n\\textbf{Symmetric Encryption} uses a single shared key for both\nencryption and decryption. It is fast and efficient but requires a\nsecure channel to exchange the key initially. \\textbf{Asymmetric\nEncryption} uses a key pair: a public key for encryption and a private\nkey for decryption. It solves the key distribution problem but is\ncomputationally more expensive.\n\nCommon algorithms include:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{AES (Advanced Encryption Standard):} A symmetric block cipher\n  widely adopted by NIST. It supports key sizes of 128, 192, and 256\n  bits.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{RSA:} A public-key cryptosystem often used for key exchange\n  and digital signatures.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC):} Provides security\n  equivalent to RSA with shorter key lengths, making it ideal for mobile\n  and IoT environments.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\[C = E_K(P)\\] \\[P = D_K^{-1}(C)\\]\n\nWhere \\(C\\) is ciphertext, \\(P\\) is plaintext, and \\(K\\) is the secret\nkey.\n\n\\paragraph{4.1.2 Hash Functions and\nIntegrity}\\label{hash-functions-and-integrity}\n\nHash functions map data of arbitrary length to a fixed-length string\n(digest). They are one-way functions, making them useful for verifying\nintegrity rather than confidentiality.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{SHA-2 and SHA-3:} Secure Hash Algorithm series used to\n  generate digests.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{MD5/SHA-1:} Considered cryptographically broken and deprecated\n  for security purposes.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\begin{quote}\n\\textbf{Real-World Scenario:} When downloading a software package,\nvendors provide a SHA-256 hash. The user downloads the hash, calculates\nthe hash of the downloaded file, and compares the two. If they match,\nthe file has not been tampered with.\n\\end{quote}\n\n\\paragraph{4.1.3 Digital Signatures and Public Key Infrastructure\n(PKI)}\\label{digital-signatures-and-public-key-infrastructure-pki}\n\nDigital signatures prove the origin of a message and ensure its\nintegrity. They rely on asymmetric encryption and hash functions.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Certificates:} Digital documents that bind a public key to an\n  entity identity (e.g., a website domain).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{PKI:} A framework using digital certificates and public key\n  certificates authorities (CA) to manage digital identities.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{TLS (Transport Layer Security):} The protocol used to secure web\ntraffic. It combines symmetric and asymmetric cryptography to establish\na secure session.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Certificate Authorities (CA):} Trusted third parties that\n  issue certificates.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{SCT (Signed Certificate Timestamps):} Used in OCSP Stapling to\n  validate revocation status efficiently.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{4.2 Authentication and Access\nControl}\\label{authentication-and-access-control}\n\nControlling who can access resources is as critical as securing the data\nitself.\n\n\\paragraph{4.2.1 Identity and Access Management\n(IAM)}\\label{identity-and-access-management-iam}\n\n\\textbf{IAM} systems manage digital identities and their access rights\nto IT resources.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol):} A standard for\n  accessing and maintaining directory information services.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Active Directory:} Microsoft’s proprietary directory service,\n  widely used in enterprise environments.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Keycloak:} An open-source Identity and Access Management\n  solution.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{4.2.2 Password Security}\\label{password-security}\n\nPasswords remain the most common credential type. However, they are\nvulnerable to brute force, rainbow table, and phishing attacks.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Mitigation:} Enforce complexity, length (12+ characters), and\n  use of password managers.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):} Requires two or more\n  factors (knowledge, possession, inherence) to authenticate. MFA is\n  essential for remote access.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{4.2.3 Federated Identity}\\label{federated-identity}\n\nOrganizations often need to share trust between systems.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OAuth 2.0:} An authorization framework allowing users to grant\n  access to third-party apps without sharing passwords.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OpenID Connect (OIDC):} A layer built on OAuth 2.0 for\n  identity verification.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{SAML (Security Assertion Markup Language):} An XML-based\n  standard for exchanging authentication data between identity providers\n  and service providers.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{JWT (JSON Web Token):} A compact URL-safe means of\n  representing claims to be transferred between two parties.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{4.3 Web and Application\nSecurity}\\label{web-and-application-security}\n\nWeb applications are frequent targets because they are exposed to the\ninternet. Security focuses on input validation and preventing common\nattack vectors.\n\n\\paragraph{4.3.1 OWASP Top 10}\\label{owasp-top-10}\n\nThe Open Web Application Security Project (OWASP) maintains a priority\nlist of security risks.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Injection:} Executing untrusted code in the system.\n  \\textbf{SQL Injection (SQLi)} occurs when database commands are\n  manipulated via user input. \\textbf{Mitigation:} Use parameterized\n  queries/prepared statements.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Broken Authentication:} Flaws allowing attackers to gain\n  unauthorized access.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Sensitive Data Exposure:} Transmission of sensitive data\n  without encryption or storage without protection.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{XSS (Cross-Site Scripting):} Injecting client-side code into\n  pages viewed by other users. \\textbf{Mitigation:} Encode output and\n  use Content Security Policy (CSP).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Insecure Configuration:} Using default credentials or enabling\n  debug modes.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{4.3.2 API Security}\\label{api-security}\n\nModern architectures rely heavily on APIs (e.g., RESTful, GraphQL).\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OWASP API Top 10:} Specifically addresses API-only\n  vulnerabilities like excessive data exposure and failure to restrict\n  access.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{API Gateways:} Centralize API management, authentication, and\n  rate limiting.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{4.4 Network Security}\\label{network-security}\n\nSecuring the network perimeter and internal infrastructure remains a\nfoundational element of defense.\n\n\\paragraph{4.4.1 Firewalls and Perimeter\nSecurity}\\label{firewalls-and-perimeter-security}\n\n\\textbf{Firewalls} inspect traffic based on rulesets.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Stateful Inspection:} Tracks the state of connections.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW):} Inspects application-layer\n  traffic and supports intrusion prevention.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{UTM (Unified Threat Management):} Combines firewall,\n  antivirus, and intrusion detection in one device.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{4.4.2 Threats to Network\nAvailability}\\label{threats-to-network-availability}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service):} An attack involving\n  multiple compromised systems attacking a target.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Mitigation:} Rate limiting, blackholing, and using Cloud-based\n  scrubbing services.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Botnets:} Networks of compromised devices used for automated\n  attacks.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{4.4.3 Monitoring and\nForensics}\\label{monitoring-and-forensics}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{IDS/IPS (Intrusion Detection/Prevention Systems):} Detect and\n  block malicious traffic.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{SIEM (Security Information and Event Management):} Aggregates\n  logs and correlates events to detect anomalies.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Wireshark/tcpdump:} Packet analysis tools for understanding\n  traffic flows and detecting protocol anomalies.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{4.5 Architectural Models and\nGovernance}\\label{architectural-models-and-governance}\n\nHow security is organized within the enterprise dictates its\neffectiveness.\n\n\\paragraph{4.5.1 Zero Trust Architecture\n(ZTA)}\\label{zero-trust-architecture-zta}\n\nZTA assumes no user or device inside the network is trustworthy.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Micro-segmentation:} Breaking the network into small zones to\n  limit lateral movement.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Identity-Centric:} Access decisions are based on user\n  identity, device health, and data sensitivity.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Just-in-Time Access:} Granting access only for the duration\n  required, rather than permanent standing permissions.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{4.5.2 Compliance and\nStandards}\\label{compliance-and-standards}\n\nOrganizations must adhere to external mandates and internal governance.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{NIST CSF (Cybersecurity Framework):} A risk management\n  framework consisting of Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and\n  Recover.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{ISO 27001:} International standard for information security\n  management systems (ISMS).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{GDPR/CCPA:} Data privacy regulations requiring consent and\n  data subject rights.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{CIS Benchmarks:} Configuration baselines for operating systems\n  and cloud services.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{4.5.3 DevSecOps}\\label{devsecops}\n\nIntegrating security into the DevOps lifecycle.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{SAST (Static Application Security Testing):} Analyzing source\n  code before build.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing):} Analyzing\n  running applications.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{SCA (Software Composition Analysis):} Scanning third-party\n  libraries for vulnerabilities.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{4.6 Data Protection\nStrategies}\\label{data-protection-strategies}\n\nProtecting data at rest, in motion, and in use.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DLP (Data Loss Prevention):} Technologies that monitor and\n  control transfer of sensitive data.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{TDE (Transparent Data Encryption):} Encrypts database files\n  without changing the application logic.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Encryption at Rest:} Using AES-256 for storage systems (e.g.,\n  BitLocker, EFS).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{4.7 Business Continuity and Incident\nResponse}\\label{business-continuity-and-incident-response}\n\nWhen security fails, the organization must survive.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{BCP (Business Continuity Planning):} Procedures to maintain\n  operations during a disruption.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DRP (Disaster Recovery Planning):} Procedures to restore IT\n  systems after a disaster.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Incident Response Lifecycle:} Preparation, Detection,\n  Containment, Eradication, Recovery, Lessons Learned.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{5. Summary \\& Actionable\nTakeaways}\\label{summary-actionable-takeaways}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Security is Holistic:} It requires a combination of\n  cryptography, access control, network hardening, and process (like\n  Incident Response).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Verify Explicitly:} Do not trust the network environment.\n  Implement Zero Trust principles where possible.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Encrypt Everything:} Assume data is stolen; ensure encryption\n  is applied to data in transit and at rest.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Automate:} Use tools like SIEM and DAST to identify issues\n  faster than manual scanning.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Audit Continuously:} Static policies are insufficient. Regular\n  auditing ensures alignment with standards like ISO 27001.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{6. Glossary of Terms}\\label{glossary-of-terms}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Access Control Model:} A framework defining how users are\n  permitted to access resources within a system. Common models include\n  Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and Attribute-Based Access Control\n  (ABAC).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Advanced Encryption Standard (AES):} A symmetric encryption\n  standard adopted by the U.S. government and industry for protecting\n  sensitive data. It uses substitution-permutation networks and supports\n  128, 192, or 256-bit key sizes.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Application-Level Encryption:} A method where data is\n  encrypted by the application layer before being transmitted to storage\n  or transmitted over the network, ensuring privacy even if storage\n  access is compromised.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Business Continuity Planning (BCP):} A process of identifying\n  risks to critical business processes and establishing policies and\n  procedures to ensure continuity during a disruption.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{CIS Security Benchmarks:} Guidelines developed by the Center\n  for Internet Security that provide configuration settings for\n  operating systems, cloud services, and applications to harden them\n  against common attacks.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Cross-Site Scripting (XSS):} A web-based attack where\n  malicious scripts are injected into trusted websites to execute in the\n  context of a user’s browser, often used to steal session cookies or\n  deface sites.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF):} An attack that tricks a\n  user into performing unwanted actions on a website where they are\n  logged in, by embedding a malicious request in a legitimate domain\n  context.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Data Loss Prevention (DLP):} A set of technologies that\n  control and monitor data transfer and storage to prevent sensitive\n  information from leaving an organization or being leaked.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Defense in Depth:} A cybersecurity strategy that employs\n  multiple layers of security controls to mitigate risk; if one layer\n  fails, others remain to provide protection.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Digital Signature:} A cryptographic mechanism that verifies\n  the authenticity and integrity of a digital document, ensuring it has\n  not been altered and originates from the claimed sender.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS):} An attack where\n  multiple compromised systems flood a target with traffic to overwhelm\n  it and cause a service outage.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Encrypted File Systems:} Systems that encrypt all data on a\n  filesystem, often used for portable drives or specific partitions to\n  prevent unauthorized access.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Firewall:} A network security device that monitors and\n  controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined\n  security rules.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Global Privacy Regulation (GDPR):} A legal framework in the EU\n  that sets strict guidelines for data processing and privacy, affecting\n  organizations globally that handle EU citizens’ data.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Identity and Access Management (IAM):} A suite of policies,\n  processes, and technologies that ensure individuals have the\n  appropriate access to resources, including user registration and\n  authentication.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Intrusion Detection System (IDS):} A tool that monitors\n  network or system traffic for malicious activity or policy violations\n  and alerts administrators.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Intrusion Prevention System (IPS):} An active security system\n  that monitors traffic and blocks malicious activity in real-time,\n  often serving as a hardened IDS.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{JSON Web Token (JWT):} An open standard for securely\n  transmitting information between parties, often used for stateless\n  authentication in APIs.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP):} A protocol used\n  to access and maintain distributed directory information services over\n  an IP network.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Load Balancer:} A device or software that distributes incoming\n  network traffic across multiple servers to ensure no single server is\n  overwhelmed.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Malware:} Software that is intentionally designed to damage or\n  exploit a computer system, network, or software.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):} A security process\n  requiring users to provide two or more verification factors to gain\n  access, such as a password and a biometric scan.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Network Security:} The practice of protecting networks from\n  unauthorized access, attacks, and disruptions through various controls\n  and protocols.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Non-Repudiation:} A security goal ensuring that a party cannot\n  deny the authenticity of their signature or actions taken.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OpenID Connect (OIDC):} An identity layer built on top of\n  OAuth 2.0 that provides authentication capabilities for clients.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OWASP Top 10:} A prioritized list of the most critical\n  security risks to web applications maintained by the Open Web\n  Application Security Project.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Packet Sniffer:} A program that intercepts and logs traffic\n  passing over a computer network, used for both analysis and malicious\n  surveillance.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Public Key Infrastructure (PKI):} A system for creating,\n  managing, and distributing digital certificates to bind public keys to\n  identities.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):} A way of managing IT\n  permissions where access is granted based on the role the user plays\n  within an organization.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) / Transport Layer Security (TLS):}\n  Protocols that provide secure communication over a computer network,\n  commonly used to secure web browsing (HTTPS).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Security Operations Center (SOC):} A centralized unit\n  responsible for an organization’s information security, monitoring,\n  analysis, and response.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Security Information and Event Management (SIEM):} A system\n  that collects, correlates, and analyzes security logs from various\n  sources to detect incidents.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Single Sign-On (SSO):} A mechanism allowing users to\n  authenticate once and gain access to multiple systems without\n  re-entering credentials.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Symmetric Cipher:} An encryption algorithm that uses the same\n  key for encryption and decryption.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Symmetric Encryption:} A method of encryption where the same\n  key is used to encrypt and decrypt data.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{TCP/IP:} A suite of communication protocols used to\n  interconnect network devices.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Threat Modeling:} The process of identifying potential\n  threats, vulnerabilities, and risks to a system to help prioritize\n  security measures.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Vulnerability Scanner:} Automated software that probes systems\n  to identify known weaknesses or misconfigurations.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA):} A security model that requires\n  explicit verification of every user and device before granting access,\n  regardless of location.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{7. Knowledge Assessment (Bloom’s Taxonomy\nBased)}\\label{knowledge-assessment-blooms-taxonomy-based}\n\n\\subsubsection{Part A: Recall \\& Comprehension (10\nMCQs)}\\label{part-a-recall-comprehension-10-mcqs}\n\n\\textbf{Q1:} Which of the following best describes the\n\\textbf{Integrity} component of the CIA Triad?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Ensuring that data is accessible when needed\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Ensuring that data is not modified or corrupted by unauthorized\n    users\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Ensuring that data is not disclosed to unauthorized parties\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Ensuring that data is encrypted at all times\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q2:} In the context of \\textbf{Asymmetric Encryption}, which key\nis used to decrypt data that was encrypted with the public key?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    The public key\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A hash function\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    The private key\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A symmetric session key\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q3:} Which OWASP vulnerability involves the execution of\nmalicious scripts within a user’s browser session?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    SQL Injection\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Broken Authentication\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q4:} What is the primary purpose of a \\textbf{Web Application\nFirewall (WAF)}?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To encrypt database storage\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To manage user identities\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To filter and monitor incoming web traffic for malicious patterns\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To accelerate website load times\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q5:} Which encryption standard is currently recommended for\nsecuring data at rest by most security guidelines?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    RC4\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    MD5\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    AES-256\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    SHA-1\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q6:} What does \\textbf{MFA} stand for in the context of\nauthentication?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Multiple File Access\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Multi-Factor Authentication\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Maximum File Array\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Manual Format Authentication\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q7:} Which of the following is a common mitigation strategy for\n\\textbf{SQL Injection} attacks?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Disabling the database server\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Using parameterized queries\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Encrypting the user browser\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Running the application locally\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q8:} A \\textbf{DDoS} attack primarily aims to impact which of\nthe CIA triad components?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Confidentiality\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Integrity\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Availability\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Authentication\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q9:} What is the main function of a \\textbf{SIEM} system?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To generate static IP addresses\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To aggregate and analyze log data from multiple sources\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To store passwords in plaintext\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To create virtual machines\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q10:} In a \\textbf{Zero Trust} model, what principle guides\naccess decisions?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Trust everyone by default\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Verify explicitly and never trust\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Allow access based on IP address alone\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Disable firewalls for speed\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q11:} A security engineer is designing a system to allow a\nthird-party mobile app to access user data without storing the user’s\npassword. Which protocol should they recommend?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Basic Auth\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    OAuth 2.0\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Telnet\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    FTP\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q12:} Which of the following is a characteristic of\n\\textbf{Symmetric Encryption}?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It requires two different keys for encryption and decryption\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It is generally faster than asymmetric encryption\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It is used for signing digital certificates\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It is immune to brute-force attacks\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q13:} When validating a downloaded file using a checksum, what\nmathematical property is primarily being checked?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Confidentiality\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Integrity\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Availability\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Authenticity\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q14:} Which framework provides a risk management approach to\ncybersecurity operations?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    ISO 27001\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    NIST CSF\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    GDPR\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    All of the above\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q15:} What is the role of a \\textbf{Ciphertext}?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Unreadable scrambled data resulting from encryption\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Readable data sent over an unencrypted connection\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A list of allowed IP addresses\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A type of malware used for ransom\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{Part B: Application \\& Analysis (5 Short Answer\nQuestions)}\\label{part-b-application-analysis-5-short-answer-questions}\n\n\\textbf{Q16:} Scenario: A web application allows users to upload profile\npictures. Users have reported that they can upload HTML pages that\nexecute JavaScript in other users’ browsers when the page loads.\n\\emph{Answer:} This is a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability. The\napplication is failing to sanitize or encode user input before rendering\nit in the browser. To mitigate this, the application must sanitize all\nuser input, encode output data within HTML contexts (like escaping\n\\textless, \\textgreater, \\&), and implement a Content Security Policy\n(CSP) to restrict the sources from which scripts can be loaded.\n\n\\textbf{Q17:} Scenario: An organization requires its employees to access\ncorporate resources from public Wi-Fi networks. The current policy\nrelies solely on a strong password. Security advisors suggest this is\ninsufficient. \\emph{Answer:} Relying only on passwords is vulnerable to\ncredential stuffing and phishing. The organization should implement\nMulti-Factor Authentication (MFA), preferably using hardware tokens or\napp-based push notifications. Additionally, establishing a corporate\ntunnel (like a secure VPN or zero trust agent) that encrypts all traffic\nbetween the employee device and the corporate network, regardless of the\nlocal network environment, is a best practice.\n\n\\textbf{Q18:} Scenario: A developer is writing a login feature. They\nwant to store passwords securely. They suggest storing the plain text\npassword in the database for ease of verification. \\emph{Answer:}\nStoring plain text passwords is a critical security failure. If the\ndatabase is breached, attackers gain direct access to user credentials.\nThe developer should store a salted hash (using algorithms like bcrypt,\nArgon2, or PBKDF2) instead. During login, the application hashes the\nuser input and compares it to the stored hash without needing the\noriginal password.\n\n\\textbf{Q19:} Scenario: You are configuring a firewall to protect a web\nserver. You want to ensure the server is not overwhelmed by a sudden\nflood of SYN packets. \\emph{Answer:} You should implement SYN Cookies\nand rate limiting on the firewall. These features prevent the firewall\nfrom allocating memory for incomplete connections. Additionally,\nenabling connection tracking and SYN flood detection in the NGFW will\ndrop malformed packets and prevent the server resource exhaustion\nassociated with a SYN flood attack.\n\n\\textbf{Q20:} Scenario: A company needs to choose between ISO 27001 and\nthe NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF). \\emph{Answer:} ISO 27001 is an\ninternational standard for ISMS that requires a certification audit,\nmaking it suitable for global companies seeking compliance recognition.\nNIST CSF is a flexible, voluntary framework often adopted by the US\nfederal sector but used broadly. NIST CSF is better for organizations\nfocused on risk management and supply chain integration (like supply\nchains), while ISO 27001 is often required for contractual obligations\nor international operations.\n\n\\subsubsection{Part C: Synthesis \\& Evaluation (2 Critical Thinking\nChallenges)}\\label{part-c-synthesis-evaluation-2-critical-thinking-challenges}\n\n\\textbf{Q21:} Design a conceptual architecture for a “Secure Remote\nWork” environment that adheres to Zero Trust principles. Describe how\nidentity, device posture, and data classification would be integrated\ninto the authentication flow. \\emph{Answer:} A robust Zero Trust remote\narchitecture involves a Cloud Access Security Broker (CASB) or Identity\nProvider (IdP) at the ingress. Every request is treated as untrusted.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\def\\labelenumi{\\arabic{enumi}.}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Identity:} The user authenticates via a federated IdP (e.g.,\n  Okta/Active Directory) with MFA.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Device Posture:} The device must meet a security baseline\n  (e.g., EDR installed, OS patched, screen lock active) before the IdP\n  grants access to the Application Access Broker.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Data Classification:} The policy checks the sensitivity of the\n  data (e.g., Public vs.~Confidential). Access to Confidential data\n  requires additional approval or lower privilege levels.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Lateral Movement:} Internal traffic is also encrypted and\n  segmented. Access from one internal app to another requires\n  re-authentication or token validation.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Outcome:} If any check fails (e.g., device is unpatched),\n  access to specific resources is denied even if the password is\n  correct.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Q22:} Evaluate the trade-offs between implementing\n\\textbf{Endpoint Encryption} (Full Disk Encryption) versus\n\\textbf{Application-Level Encryption} for a banking application. Which\nwould you recommend for a banking application and why? \\emph{Answer:}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Endpoint Encryption (FDE):} Protects data at rest if the\n  physical device is lost or stolen. However, it does not protect data\n  while in use (RAM) or if the user has physical access to the device\n  (e.g., USB drive). It introduces performance overhead.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Application-Level Encryption:} Protects data regardless of\n  where it is stored. If an admin accesses a database directly (which\n  they sometimes do for debugging), the data is unreadable without the\n  key. However, it relies heavily on key management complexity.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Recommendation:} For a banking application, a hybrid approach\n  is usually best, but application-level encryption is often prioritized\n  for the transaction data (PII) because administrative access is a\n  major risk vector. Endpoint encryption should be enabled for device\n  compliance (BYOD policies). Application-level encryption ensures that\n  even if the storage system is compromised, the business logic (key\n  management) protects the core secrets. However, full disk encryption\n  is a non-negotiable baseline for hardware loss protection. Therefore,\n  \\textbf{both} are recommended, but application-level encryption\n  provides a higher logical security posture against insider threats and\n  admin access.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\end{document}\n",
